To start with the conclusion, I personally think that “Four Seas” is a complete mess, in every sense of the word.
But if you say that this film has no highlights, it is not true. At least the soundtrack is very good.
There are many kinds of bad movies. A movie that makes you fall asleep in the cinema is one kind. A movie that makes you feel angry after watching is another kind. For the audience, “Four Seas” is like a monkey on Mount Emei. It can ignite your anger in countless ways.
Han Han is really talented in insulting the audience.
The most painful thing about this film is that it seems to be a creature in the novel of the Ke family, indescribable, and full of holes like the trench coat on Xiao Mago.
Even after you've seen it, you wonder if there's some kind of metaphor in there that the director is trying to hide, but when you think about it, it's just a pile of shit.
Let me start with some of the plot points that I found very confusing.
The male protagonist should live in some small island in the south and depend on his grandmother.
Her grandmother had a wish to see snow, so the plot arranged for an old man in the neighborhood to drive her to the northeast to see snow.
An elderly person takes a minibus from the south to the northeast to see the snow?
He and his father were very confident about it.
And then the old man was never heard from again.
How did the female protagonist's brother get a business license to open a hotel on an abandoned ship?
Why can the small gang of motorcyclists on the island easily block a mountain road?
Then, the truck that delivers steel bars in the middle of the road stopped by the roadside, and the truck driver was nowhere to be found.
This prop sense is too obvious.
Why would a fly-by-night gangster with a bad credit history and a suspicion of illegal activities become an auxiliary police officer?
Why didn’t she call the police when she was faced with high-interest loans? The police in the movie even asked her if she was in trouble.
An adult who knows how to use a smartphone, doesn't know how to open a hotel room door with a key card, then forcibly arranges to stay overnight outside a hotel room door without finding the hotel owner?
The nonsensical plot is all over the film and it's so ridiculous that I feel pain when I recall them.
Beyond the plot, what's even worse is the film's main theme, or lack thereof, which is a hideous stitched monster.
At the beginning of the film, you might think he wanted to make a comedy, but after 20 minutes, you might guess that it could be an inspirational film, and halfway through the film, you might think that it might be a drama about the struggles of young people in small towns. After watching the film, you would have no idea what you had watched, and then you would doubt yourself deeply, wondering why you went to the cinema on New Year's Day to watch a film about defecation.
For most people, they are also very familiar with the patterns of all kinds of movies. For example, if you want to shoot a motorcyclist, then you will be motivated and funny, and tell the audience how a talented rookie can step by step towards success.
If you want to make a love film, then you should honestly tell the story of the love and hate between the young man who likes riding a motorcycle and the waitress.
If you want to reflect reality, then at least your plot should not have things that are contrary to the normal world.
(For example, the character played by Yin Zheng, whose new motorcycle was just burned, and who can sing) cannot be used to open some brainstorming that even Kennedy cannot withstand.
As for the female lead, who is not mentally retarded, but behaves like a 5-year-old in some aspects, I really don’t know whether it is due to the acting or the director’s requirement.
I also don't understand why Han Han, as a race car driver, is so keen on adding a car crash scene in his own movie.
Is it to annoy the audience that such a film is scheduled for the Spring Festival?
