South African doctor: Omicron is highly contagious, but most are mild cases
Many doctors and medical experts in South Africa believe that the Omicron variant of the coronavirus causes milder symptoms than the Delta variant, although it is transmitted faster.
According to the Associated Press, as the Omicron variant spreads in South Africa, a medical organization of more than 5,000 doctors in Gauteng Province reported that they currently treated mild symptoms of coronavirus patients, such as body pain and coughs similar to flu.
Dr Unben Pillay, the organisation's director, said he was seeing dozens of patients a day but none had yet needed hospitalisation.
Pillay said, "They can control the disease at home, and most people recover within 10 to 14 days of isolation. "
He said this included older patients and those with health problems who were more likely to become seriously ill from coronavirus infection.
He said that in comparison, during the last wave of Delta infections, he treated COVID-19 patients who "were struggling to breathe and had low oxygen levels.
Many people will be hospitalized within a few days. "
Within two weeks of the first report of the Omicron variant in southern Africa, other doctors shared similar experiences.
Although it will take several weeks to collect enough data, doctors' observations and early evidence provide some clues.
According to the National Institute for Communicable Diseases of South Africa, among the COVID-19 patients hospitalized in recent weeks, only 30% were severe cases, which is half of the proportion of severe cases in the previous wave; the average length of hospital stay was shortened from 8 days to 2.8 days; among the recent COVID-19 patients, only 3% died, while the death rate in the early outbreak in South Africa reached 20%.
"Right now, almost all signs point to it being a mild disease," said Willem Hanekom, director of the African Institute for Health Research, citing data from the National Institute for Communicable Diseases and other reports.
He also said, "It is still early and we need to get later data.
Usually, hospitalization and death occur later, and we have been in this wave of the epidemic for only two weeks. "
Meanwhile, scientists around the world are observing case numbers and hospitalization rates while testing the effectiveness of current vaccines and treatment methods.
While Delta remains the dominant coronavirus strain worldwide, cases of Omicron are being reported in dozens of countries, with South Africa at the epicenter.
Gauteng has seen a 400% increase in new cases in the first week of December, with more than 90% of cases now testing positive for the Omicron variant, health officials said.
