The International Olive Oil Council classifies olive oil into two major categories, virgin olive oil and lampante olive oil or refined olive oil, with five levels.
Virgin olive oil, also known as natural olive oil, is the oil obtained directly from fresh olives by mechanical cold pressing and then filtered to remove impurities.
They are divided into three levels according to their acidity:
Extra Virgin Olive Oil: This is the highest grade and best quality olive oil, which is a pure natural product.
It has a great taste, a mild and pleasant plant aroma, and an acidity of less than 1%.
Fine virgin olive oil: Slightly higher acidity, but not more than 2%, pure and aromatic taste.
Ordinary Virgin: taste and flavor are acceptable, acidity not exceeding 3.3%.
Refined olive oil is olive oil that has been refined from virgin olive oil with an acidity level greater than 3.3% or becomes "second oil".
Refined olive oil comes in two grades:
Olive Oil: Refined olive oil mixed with a certain percentage of virgin olive oil to moderate the taste and color, with an acidity of less than 1.5% and a transparent pale golden color.
Refined Olive-Pomace Oil: It is extracted from oil residue by dissolution and refined.
If you've read the classification, you should have found that the acidity of olive oil is a very important indicator.
It is known that olives are like grapes used to make wine. Olives from different regions and different olive varieties will produce olive oil of different standards.
Best places to grow olives:
The Mediterranean coast has the most suitable soil and climate conditions such as temperature, sunshine, air humidity and precipitation required for the growth of oil olives.
Outside of the Mediterranean coast, it can only be planted sporadically in suitable areas.
Main production areas (Mediterranean):
Italy, Spain, Greece, Turkey, Portugal, Morocco, Syria, Israel, Tunisia, Algeria, Egypt, Pakistan, Lebanon, and so on, and similar climates are found in Argentina, Peru, South Africa, and so on, far south of the equator.
Olive harvesting month:
From March to June every year, it is the time for the olive tree to bloom and bear fruit. From July to October, the fruits begin to develop and mature, and the fruits transition from green to red, purple, and finally black.
November to February is the harvest season (freshness of olive oil is important).
Nutritional composition of olive oil:
Olive oil is rich in a variety of monounsaturated fatty acids, namely oleic acid and linoleic acid, as well as vitamins A, B, D, E, K and antioxidants.
Olive oil is considered to be the most suitable oil for human nutrition among all oils discovered so far.
Biden says 'fire of freedom' will never be extinguished as democracy summit ends
The online "Summit for Democracy" convened by U.S. President Biden, attended by government leaders, civil society and private sector leaders from 110 countries around the world, ended on Friday (December 10).
In his closing remarks, Biden said that the flame of liberty burns in the hearts of every person in the world, and that it can never be extinguished by authoritarian regimes.
He also said that he plans to hold a second global "democracy summit" next year.
Representatives of the Taiwan government, including Councilor Tang Fenghe, and Hong Kong democracy and human rights activist Luo Guancheng, who is currently in exile in the UK, also spoke at the summit the next day.
"We know how much work lies ahead of us," Biden said.
But we also know that we can meet the challenge, because, as I said before, the democratic world is everywhere, as this summit has shown.
The autocratic regime can never extinguish the spark of freedom, which burns in the hearts of people all over the world. "
Biden also said that defending democracy requires the joint efforts of the whole society.
He said, "As the leaders of the government, we have the responsibility to listen to our citizens, strengthen the fence of democracy, promote reform, realize transparent accountability governance, and be more resistant to the power of the buffer and impact of authoritarianism, as well as those who want to pursue power nakedly before the public interest. "
The themes of the second day of the "Democracy Summit" are defending human rights, consolidating democracy and opposing authoritarianism, and fighting against digital authoritarianism and affirming democratic values.
This day coincides with the "International Human Rights Day".
UN Secretary-General António Guterres also said in his opening remarks that the cause of democracy faces challenges worldwide, especially those brought about by emerging digital technologies.
He said, "It is time to reaffirm our common values and positions, as well as the resilience of democracy.
This requires deepening social dialogue, addressing inequality, fighting corruption, renewing the social contract, investing in quality education and expanding opportunities for women and girls.
This also requires resolutely condemning all forms of populism, nativism and extremism, including the use of all tools provided by the technological revolution. "
Hong Kong pro-democracy and human rights activist Luo Guansheng addressed the summit through a pre-recorded video, reminding participants to continue to pay attention to the human rights situation in Hong Kong and ethnic minorities in China such as Uyghurs and Tibetans.
He said, "For a long time, the world has accepted China's rise without establishing a mechanism to hold it accountable.
This is why politicians and civil society leaders must work together.
We must use everything we have to ensure that the revival of democracy is our top priority. "
He also called on the free world to unite against the threat of the CCP.
He said: "Maybe some of you are afraid to anger President Xi Jinping, maybe some of you don't want to lose the Chinese market, or maybe some of you haven't realized the threat that the Communist Party poses to our democratic values.
And that's why we failed.
This is also why we must change. "
Taiwan's Executive Council Secretary-General Tang Feng released a "National Statement" through video at a roundtable discussion on "Combating Digital Despotism and Affirming Democratic Values", emphasizing that Taiwan is standing at the forefront of the fight against despotism and authoritarianism.
He also shared his experience of combating the novel coronavirus pneumonia through information transparency and building mutual trust.
He said, "At a time when Taiwan's democracy is still in its infancy, we have always stood firmly at the forefront of the global fight against authoritarianism, and we have played a leading role in promoting global freedom, democracy, and human rights.
During the pandemic, authoritarian governments around the world have rationalized human rights violations in the name of public health and collective interest, and there are signs that global democracy has regressed.
However, in Taiwan, we do not need to seal off cities to control the epidemic, and we do not need to take down the platform to defeat false information. "
While the second day of the "democracy summit" was going on, the US Treasury Department announced that it would impose sanctions on 15 individuals and 10 entities in China, North Korea and other countries that were found to have seriously violated human rights, and imposed investment restrictions on a Chinese company that was found to have used surveillance technology to assist in the violation of human rights.
