I want to try tennis, but I am afraid that I can't keep up with it, so I want to find a similar price to practice first.
Unfriendly, in fact, price is often directly proportional to friendliness, and if you can't tell, then the expensive ones are normally more friendly than the cheap ones, and these "entry-level" ones are actually industrial waste.
They are very weak, and the ball vibrates very much when it hits the racket. Hard balls cannot withstand them, and even experts have difficulty controlling them.
The logic and subtext of their existence is that tennis is so difficult, and we know you can’t learn it, and we know you don’t want to buy expensive ones, so we’ll make a cheap one to fool you, and you’ll only play with it twice, and then you won’t play it anymore, so this racket is cheap, and you won’t feel guilty.
When you can really tell the difference between good and bad, it means that you have made progress, so come back to me when you want to buy something expensive, and I will make money twice.
It is recommended to spend a similar amount of money to buy a better second-hand racket, and choose prince or Dunlop. These two brands are not well-known, so good rackets do not sell for much. A new racket costs 500-600 yuan, and a second-hand one costs 200 yuan. Isn't it great to enjoy a thousand-yuan racket?
It's quite friendly to beginners, but it's useless after the beginner stage, but most people, especially girls, can't pass the beginner stage.
