Xinhua Plus | Beijing Youth Ice Hockey League Ignites Winter Olympic Enthusiasm - Xinhuanet
On December 11, Beijing Dreaming ING player Pang Ya Tian (center) took a shot during the game.
On that day, the 2021-2022 season Beijing Youth Ice Hockey Club League continued to compete at major ice rinks in Beijing.
This league is divided into regular season and playoffs. The age range of participating athletes is from U6 to U18. The group setting is divided into competitive groups and selection groups, attracting nearly 3,600 players from 256 teams in the city.
After more than a decade of development, the Beijing Youth Hockey Club League has become a platform for young ice hockey fans in Beijing to exchange skills and make friends, and has also provided excellent players for the Beijing Youth Ice Hockey Team and the Youth Team.
The league will play a role in promoting ice hockey among young people in Beijing and igniting the passion for ice hockey at the Winter Olympics, laying a foundation for the development of ice hockey in Beijing after the Winter Olympics.
Girl becomes target to raise money for sister: "I thought it was just flesh wounds" - Xinhuanet
A girl in Hangzhou Wulin Square stood in front of a large wooden board, on which was written "human target, 10 yuan for one arrow".
Yesterday morning, a photo of “Hangzhou girl willingly become a ‘target’ for her sister’s fundraising” went viral on WeChat.
The photo shows that a girl in Hangzhou Wulin Square stands in front of a big wooden board which is slightly higher than her head and writes "human target, 10 yuan for one arrow" on it to indicate passers-by can pay money and use bow and arrow to shoot.
This behavior has sparked heated discussion among netizens.
Yesterday evening, the propaganda officer of Xiacheng District Public Security Bureau of Hangzhou City confirmed to the Beijing News reporter that the police confirmed that the girl made such a radical action after her big sister raised donations. However, this behavior is not worth advocating. After being warned, the girl left the local police station yesterday.
Girl who raised money by being a 'human target' on the street warned
In the online pictures, a long-haired girl stood in front of a large wooden board in the shape of "a", and the words "human target, 10 yuan for one arrow" were written on the wooden board with black ink. Another wooden board in front of her had her bank account number, WeChat, Alipay account number, etc.
The image sparked controversy online.
Some netizens believed that this was "a begging behavior to attract attention", while many netizens expressed sympathy for it.
"We found this situation around 10 am on the morning of October 23 when we were patrolling near the square. There were already quite a few people watching. "
The police officers of Tianshui Police Station near the incident site said in an interview with local media that there were two girls who set up stalls, both about 20 years old and dressed in a youthful and beautiful way.
One of the girls who was made to be the "human target" kept crying and begged passers-by to play this game; another girl helped to call out, saying that her friend's sister had leukemia and now needed money for surgery, asking everyone to help.
"We contacted the local police in Guizhou and learned that the girl was raising money for her sister's leukemia treatment. "
Last night, the publicity officer of Hangzhou Public Security Bureau Xiacheng Branch told Beijing News that the girl was named Ji Jiali, and she was collecting donations for her sister Ji Jiayan.
However, because the treatment still requires more than 300,000 yuan, and no good way to raise funds was found, such behavior was done.
He said that during the whole process, the two girls acted as targets and one was responsible for shooting, but did not participate in the process. No one was found shooting arrows during the whole process. Only someone watched and took photos and posted them on Weibo.
After that, the police found out through the public security department that the bow and arrow used was not under control.
The person in charge said, "We do not advocate this kind of eye-catching fundraising method."
After being warned by the police station officers, she has left the police station.
