Vietnam News - Gulf Cooperation Council should meet common challenges
The stops of Saudi Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman include Oman, the United Arab Emirates (UAE), Qatar, Bahrain and Kuwait.
It is worth noting that this is the first visit of Crown Prince Mohammed to Qatar since Saudi Arabia and its Arab allies imposed an embargo on Qatar in mid-2017, leading to a Gulf diplomatic crisis.
In this crisis, Saudi Arabia, the United Arab Emirates, Bahrain and Egypt broke off diplomatic relations with Qatar and imposed a comprehensive embargo on it, accusing Qatar of supporting terrorist organizations and having close ties with Iran.
The diplomatic tensions have seriously affected the internal unity of the Gulf Cooperation Council and have had a negative impact on the economies of the countries in the region.
Four Arab states have blocked land, sea and air routes to Qatar.
The crisis was resolved only at the January 2021 Gulf Cooperation Council summit, where the Gulf states signed an agreement to move towards "unity and stability" mediated by the United States and Kuwait.
Saudi Arabia has reopened its border with Qatar.
Saudi Arabia and Egypt are taking the lead in trying to restore relations with Qatar, having appointed new ambassadors there.
The Gulf Arab states' efforts to resolve their differences and restore ties with Qatar are driven by the need to unite behind the interests of their countries and the stability of the region, and to confront common challenges.
During the visit of the Saudi Arabian Crown Prince to Oman, the two sides agreed to open the first direct land route between the two Gulf neighbors.
The 725-kilometer-long corridor connecting the two countries will facilitate the travel of citizens and the integration of supply chains.
Saudi Arabia's Public Investment Fund (PIF) will invest $5 billion in Oman, and the two countries' enterprises signed 13 memorandums of understanding worth $30 billion.
The visit of the Saudi Crown Prince to the Gulf countries coincided with the negotiations between Iran and major world powers to save the Iran nuclear agreement, known as the Joint Comprehensive Plan of Action (JCPOA), which the Arab Gulf countries condemned because they believed it could not resolve the missile issue with Iran.
The Gulf countries have always regarded Iran as a long-term "competitor" and regarded Iran's nuclear program as a "threat" to regional security.
The Gulf countries believe that it is flawed not to address the issue of Iranian missiles and not to take action to curb Iranian activities in the region.
Saudi Arabia has engaged in direct talks with Iran against the backdrop of the failure of the major powers and Iran to meet the aspirations of the Gulf countries in the nuclear talks.
Its goal is to prevent the escalation of tensions in the region.
This is one of the factors that have slowed down the economic growth of the Gulf Cooperation Council countries.
The upcoming GCC summit will be the first since the end of some member states' disputes with Qatar.
This is an opportunity for the Gulf Cooperation Council to re-evaluate its diplomatic achievements in promoting the restoration of GCC unity and propose measures to ensure full cooperation among member states to meet common challenges and contribute to the stability and development of the Gulf countries.
