Asian American group files complaint against Yale and other Ivy League schools for admissions discrimination - Xinhuanet
Xinhua News Agency, Washington, May 23 (Reporter Xu Jianmei) More than 130 Asian-American groups filed a complaint with the U.S. Department of Education and the Justice Department on May 23, protesting against discrimination against Asian-American applicants in the enrollment process by three Ivy League universities - Yale University, Brown University and Dartmouth College.
The Asian Education Alliance, which led the appeal, held a press conference in Washington on the same day.
Zhao Yukong, the chairman of the group, said at the press conference that in the past 10 years, the number of Asian applicants who meet the admission requirements of American prestigious universities has doubled, but among these three American prestigious universities, the proportion of Asians has remained basically the same.
Many studies have shown that these universities "systematically and persistently discriminate against Asian applicants" in the so-called "comprehensive consideration" admissions process.
Zhao Yunkong said that in 2014, Sanders and other scholars’ research showed that among these three universities, Asian students had the lowest acceptance rate compared to the number of applicants in all ethnic groups.
Data from the U.S. Department of Education also shows that Brown University and Dartmouth College have the lowest acceptance rates for Asian students among all Ivy League schools, and Yale University and Brown University have implemented de facto racial quotas for Asian applicants for more than 20 years.
Zhao Yukong told Xinhua News Agency that they expect the U.S. Department of Education and Justice to launch an investigation after submitting the appeal, and take measures to prevent these universities from continuing to discriminate against Asians in enrollment after finding out the facts.
They also expect the colleges that have been complained to take this issue seriously and change their admission policies for Asian students.
At present, the three universities complained of have not yet responded.
The Asian Coalition for Educational Equity was established in 2015, and had complained of Harvard University's enrollment discrimination, but the US Department of Education did not accept it on procedural reasons.
In addition to the American Chinese community, the American Indian, Korean, Japanese and Pakistani education groups also participated in the appeal.
