3,200 newborn babies in Sabah have no birth certificates due to special anti-counterfeiting paper out of stock
(Kota Kinabalu, 11th) Sabah National Registration Department said that since the supply of special anti-counterfeiting paper was exhausted, it has been unable to issue birth certificates to newborns since early October, and the number of affected people has reached 3,200.
However, the director of the bureau, Kieruaminus, promised that all babies who have applied for birth certificates have been recorded in the system, and the certificates will be reissued after the anti-counterfeit paper arrives next week.
He made an explanation at a press conference in response to a report by a online media that Sabah's birth certificate printing had been a problem for up to 10 months.
He said that the problem had occurred two months ago, and Sarawak also faced the same problem.
"One reason is that the Chinese manufacturer of special anti-counterfeiting paper was affected by the epidemic and delayed delivery to our country. "
He explained that the anti-counterfeiting paper used in Sabah's birth certificate was different from other regions, so it could not be borrowed from other states.
Kerawamunu revealed that the latest batch of anti-counterfeiting paper had arrived in Kuala Lumpur on June 6 and was handed over to the printing company for partial printing work on June 8.
"The bureau will send someone to Kuala Lumpur to pick up the goods next Thursday, and then return to Sabah to carry out the final stage of printing. It is estimated that it will take two more working days (December 20) to hand them over to the applicants. "
"We will notify the applicants to go to the local sub-bureaus to get the birth registration papers. "
He added that Sabah has issued 24,801 birth certificates so far this year.
