I just read an article about menstruation. It's too long.
Let me give you a little science lesson for the men who don’t know.
Most girls will experience their first period somewhere between 13 and 15 years old, and will stop menstruating around age 50.
After that, if you are healthy, you will have a menstrual period once a month regularly.
But it is not so regular that it can be accurately predicted. It is about a week or so, and you need to prepare in advance. Sometimes, if you are unlucky, you will have an accident (suddenly a few days in advance).
The length of time, amount of blood and severity of cramps vary from person to person. Some people have it for three days, some for a week; some people bleed little, some have heavy bleeding; some people don't react at all, some people are in so much pain that they are taken away in an ambulance. The severity of cramps changes over time, so just because you didn't have them before doesn't mean you won't have them forever.
Menstrual periods are uncontrollable, bleeding 24 hours a day for days at a time from start to finish, and all you can do is use sanitary napkins or tampons to absorb the blood so it doesn't drip on your clothes and chairs.
Dysmenorrhea is mainly due to the secretion of prostaglandins, and taking ibuprofen quickly at the beginning of menstruation can effectively relieve the pain of that month. Taking it after the onset of dysmenorrhea is useless.
It is easy to have diarrhea before menstruation due to uterine congestion and excessive prostaglandin release.
If you have a period and suddenly get diarrhea, you will probably have your period within two days.
It is best to change the sanitary napkin every two or three hours. Otherwise, bacteria will easily breed. Apart from sleeping time, the worst case is to change it six times a day, seven days a month, which is 42 pieces. If you add the pieces prepared in advance, there may be 50 pieces, and not one piece every month.
(Here's a modification, tampons don't have to be changed often, about five or six hours, up to eight hours)
Hormones during menstruation may make you more irritable, and ovulation may make you moody because of the hormonal changes in your body. It is just bad luck, and your body is playing tricks on you.
But official crime figures show that women are still very good at controlling their emotions and don't make excuses for emotional problems.
It is not just blood that flows, but also the detached endometrium.
The endometrium is the thinnest after the menstruation, and then it becomes thicker and thicker. It can be understood that the uterus is a house. Every month, a house is built (the endometrium becomes thicker) to prepare to welcome guests. If the guests do not come, the whole house is demolished (menstruation is discharged). A new house is built next month, and this cycle repeats.
Menstruation is very inconvenient. For several decades, you will bleed for 24 hours every month, and you will also have abdominal pain and lower back pain. Who would want that?
But a person is born because his mother had her period, and the period was to prepare the house where you will stay for 10 months when you first come to this world, so everyone should be thankful that their mothers have regular and healthy periods.
In the past, many things about women were suppressed, and society brainwashed women to say that they were dirty and shameful, and everyone thought it was so. But times have changed.
If someone thinks that menstruation is disgusting, that the female body's preparation for life is disgusting, and that it shouldn't be talked about in public, then girls stand up, don't think that way anymore, and don't let men who have never experienced it, and will never experience it, and don't know much about it, be so ungrateful and unappreciative. How can you face your mother, how can you think about your own child, with such an attitude?
Lastly, ladies, try tampons, they really work.
