Metaphysics is the study of questions that cannot be answered through direct perception, using rational reasoning and logic.
Metaphysics is the fundamental principle of philosophy and aims to explain the basic nature of existence and the world.
The Chinese translation of “metaphysics” comes from the I Ching “Form is above, and is called the Tao; form is below, and is called the vessel”, which was translated from metaphysic by the Japanese philosopher Ikeda Tetsujiro (Meiji era).
Metaphysical branches include ontology, natural theology and its extended problems.
Ontology is "the study of which terms represent real entities and which terms represent concepts".
Metaphysical naturalism is the denial of God's existence.
Its theoretical basis is that all phenomena can be explained by the concept of natural reason, and it was proposed by philosophers who supported science and evolution.
Methodological naturalism is a research approach that says nothing about God.
Spiritual philosophy is a branch of philosophy that studies the nature of consciousness and its relationship to the body, particularly the brain.
Dualism and monism are the two main schools of thought on the relationship between mind and body.
Epistemology is a branch of philosophy that investigates the nature, origins, and scope of knowledge.
Ethics is the discipline of systematic thought and study of the moral life of human beings; here, morality is defined as all the codes of conduct that are accepted by a group of people or a culture.
It is divided into meta-ethics (the abstract study of the nature of ethical theories or critiques) and normative ethics (the evaluation of different moral views and the giving of moral prescriptions about what is right or wrong to do.
It studies what moral codes people should follow), applied ethics (the academic study of applying ethical theories to real-life situations, including bioethics and political ethics), and descriptive ethics (gathering information about how people live, summarizing observed patterns.
It is not an examination of ethics from a preconceived theory but rather an investigation of actual moral agents and their real choices, all the moral factors that lead to these choices, and the ethical views held by social groups.
The history of ethics includes Greek and Roman ethics, and medieval Christian ethics.
Part I: Humanistic ethics, represented by Valla and Montaigne.
Part II: The ethical thought of the Reformation, represented by Calvin.
Part 3: Political ethics, represented by Machiavelli and others, modern ethics (17-18th century, British empiricist ethics, represented by Hume and others, 17-18th century, Continental rationalist ethics, represented by Spinoza and others, 18th century, French Enlightenment ethics, represented by Voltaire and others, 18-19th century, German academic ethics, represented by Kant and Hegel and others, 18-19th century, British utilitarian ethics, represented by Bentham and Sidgwick and others, 19th century, British evolutionary ethics, represented by Spencer and Huxley and others, late 19th century, German neo-Kantian ethics, represented by Liebknecht and others, late 19th century, neo-Hegelian ethics, represented by Thomas Hill Green and others), transitional period ethics (irrationalist ethics, represented by Kierkegaard, Schopenhauer and Nietzsche and others).
Logic is the study of the principles and standards of valid reasoning and proof.
Logic usually includes three parts: inductive reasoning, abductive reasoning, and deductive reasoning, and can be divided into formal logic and informal logic.
Aesthetics is a discipline that is concerned with the study of the nature and meaning of beauty.
