In the past two years, the blockade and encirclement of Huawei chips by the United States has not only put Huawei in an unprecedented predicament, but also caused huge losses to "American" enterprises.
The United States suppresses China's chip industry and requires its own enterprises and relevant enterprises of its allies to block China's chip industry at the same time, which directly leads to the consequences that the orders and profits of global chip suppliers are affected, resulting in the research and development investment and production capacity of chip production enterprises decreasing, and thus causing the shortage and disorder of the global chip supply chain.
Now, “chip shortage” has affected all major industries around the world, such as car manufacturers shutting down production and mobile phones becoming more expensive. Almost every industry that uses chips is suffering from this problem.
In order to master the "chip" autonomy, more and more technology giants in many countries have begun to take the path of "de-Americanization".
In order to firmly control the global semiconductor control and restrict the development of China's semiconductor industry, the United States has recently taken frequent actions, such as hosting the "Chip Summit", inviting 64 countries to establish a semiconductor alliance, and allocating funds to support the US local technology giants. For example, in order to regain its leading position in the chip field, the old American technology giant Intel spent $20 billion to build two wafer processing plants in Arizona, USA, and opened up the chip foundry business.
Most people thought that Intel's move would have some impact on TSMC and would likely take away TSMC's "job".
This is because Intel, as a long-established technology giant in the United States, has been deeply involved in the semiconductor industry for decades, has a large number of talent teams and technical reserves, and is sure to rise quickly in the short term and defeat Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Company.
However, the founder of TSMC, Zhang Zhongmou, did not care about Intel's decision.
At the “2021 Master Think Tank Forum” held in April this year, Zhang Chunmei made a startling remark. He said without hesitation about Intel’s move to build a wafer factory: It is quite ironic that Intel builds a chip foundry.
Zhang Chongmou despised Intel’s entry into the wafer field because he still harbored a grudge against TSMC and Intel’s feud more than 30 years ago.
In the 1960s and 1970s, Intel has always been the brightest star in the manufacturing industry of the United States, occupying the top position in the world, and is the world's semiconductor leader.
By the 1980s, the world's high-end semiconductor chips had been in the hands of a few powerful companies.
At that time, the semiconductor industry was characterized by the IDM model, and chips were designed, manufactured and packaged by a single factory. Intel was the representative of that era.
However, such a model has overwhelmed some small businesses, and many semiconductor entrepreneurs have been deterred by huge production line construction costs.
Under the call of market demand, after entering the 1980s, the chip industry began to show signs of division of labor and cooperation. In 1984, Zhang Chongshi heard that there was a company that specialized in chip design. He believed that since there was a company that specialized in design, a factory that specialized in wafer manufacturing could be established.
Soon after, Zhang Chongmei’s vision became a reality, and TSMC was born.
TSMC focuses on the single link of "wafer processing" as its main business and does not engage in design but only provides wafer manufacturing services.
At that time, the newly established Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Company was just a small player in the face of the giant Intel.
In order to obtain capital support, Zhang Chongmou once sought investment from Intel, but at that time, Intel, after hearing that TSMC only did the manufacturing link of the chip industry, was obviously very arrogant and looked down on TSMC, and resolutely refused to invest in TSMC.
Chung also believed that Intel at that time did not take wafer process services seriously.
Unexpectedly, the giants who looked down upon wafer manufacturing services in the past are now also starting to do wafer foundry.
So, Zhang Zhonglou felt that Intel entering wafer fabrication was ironic.
Today, TSMC’s market value has surpassed that of Intel.
Faced with TSMC’s aggressive attack, Intel naturally did not dare to stand still.
Now, TSMC has rushed towards 3nm chips, while Intel is still stuck at 14nm seven years ago.
To catch up is not easy.
