Starry Sky Appointment | The Geminid Meteor Shower Will Reach Its Maximum on the 14th - Xinhuanet
Xinhua News Agency, Tianjin, December 12 (Reporter Zhou Rujian) According to astronomical science popularization experts, as the last large-scale meteor shower of the year, the Geminid meteor shower, one of the three major meteor showers in the Northern Hemisphere, will reach its maximum on December 14. Friends who like meteor showers can observe it in the latter half of the night.
A double star meteor shower photographed by Beijing astronomy enthusiast An Jiu in Qinghai on December 13, 2018.
Most meteor showers are caused by periodic comets, but the parent body of the Geminid meteor shower is the asteroid 3200 Phaethon, an Apollo asteroid.
The Geminid meteor shower is also the second known meteor shower that is not "nurtured" by a comet.
Xiu Lipeng, a council member of Tianjin Astronomical Society and an astronomy popularization expert, said that just like the New Year’s blockbuster, the Geminid meteor shower generally appears at the end of the year, and its “release period” can last from December 4 to 17.
This meteor shower is known for its large and stable flow, and together with Quadrantid meteor shower and Perseid meteor shower, it is called the three major meteor showers in the Northern Hemisphere.
One of the most notable features of the Geminid meteor shower is that the meteors move slowly and have rich colors, as well as many fireballs, which are suitable for both visual observation and photography by beginners in astronomy.
Tianjin astronomy enthusiast Yang Jing took a picture of the Geminid meteor shower in Tianjin Jizhou on December 13, 2020.
Astronomical forecasts show that the Geminid meteor shower will reach its maximum around 15:00 on December 14 this year, with a maximum ZHR (the number of meteors that can be seen per hour under ideal observation conditions, with the radiant point located directly above the head) of 150.
Recommended index map of the Geminid meteor shower.
"The 'falling stars' of the Gemini meteor shower last year left a deep impression on people, but this year's Gemini meteor shower will be affected by moonlight and its brilliance will be slightly reduced.
The 14th is the 11th day of the 11th month in the lunar calendar, and the moon phase is the gibbous moon. The bright moonlight will interfere with observation. Fortunately, the moon will set after midnight, and at this time, the radiant point of the Gemini meteor shower will rise higher.
Friends who like meteor showers can observe them in the last few hours after the moon sets in the second half of the night, but they should pay attention to keeping warm. "
How can we observe as many meteors as possible?
"It is not recommended to observe in cities. Choose a high altitude, low light pollution and open view area facing northeast in suburbs or villages, then wait patiently for the moon to set and observe with the naked eye.
It should be noted that the Geminid meteor shower has a large flow in all directions, so there is no need to keep staring at the radiant point of the Geminid meteor shower during observation.
For those who like photography, if you want to take a brilliant "meteor shower film", it is best to choose a wide-angle, large aperture lens, and use high ISO and maximum aperture when shooting.
Based on stability and continuity considerations, tripods, spare batteries and shutter release cables are also essential. "
Xiu Leping warned.
What is the difference between laser TV and traditional TV in terms of market positioning?
In my understanding, the traditional TV referred to by the author should be LCD TV. The differences between laser TV and LCD TV in market positioning mainly include the following two points:
Firstly, laser TVs focus more on large screen markets.
Compared with the comprehensive market positioning of LCD TVs, laser TVs are obviously more focused on the large screen market, or laser TVs are a product born for large screens.
At present, the size of mainstream LCD TVs is mostly between 55 inches and 65 inches, while the size of laser TVs is generally above 75 inches, and the largest is more than 100 inches, which fully meets users' demand for large-screen color TVs.
In recent years, the shipment and sales volume of laser TVs have been increasing worldwide, accounting for more than 60% of the overall TV sales volume of 80 inches and above.
According to the prediction of CIC, in 2020, the sales volume of laser TV market will increase by more than 140% and continue to maintain high growth.
The reason for such good results in the large screen market is mainly due to the excellent performance of laser TV in protecting eyesight.
Based on the imaging principle of diffuse reflection of laser TV, the light emitted by the laser light source does not directly enter the eyeball. Compared with the backlight source of LCD TV, the imaging process is closer to the imaging process of objects under natural conditions, and the stimulation to the eyes is also smaller.
The China Electronics Standardization Institute’s Sai Xi Laboratory and Beijing Union Medical College Hospital’s ophthalmology experts obtained 144 sets of test data through strict comparative experiments under the same conditions for watching laser TVs and LCD TVs.
Data confirmed that laser TVs do indeed have strong eye protection characteristics and even have certain improvement effects in some aspects.
In an era when vision health is increasingly important, laser TVs have attracted more and more users with their eye protection advantages, and have opened up another breakthrough in the TV market.
Second, laser TVs are more focused on the high-end market.
In contrast to the continuous decline in the price of LCD TVs, the current market price of laser TVs is mostly above 10,000 yuan, and the price gradually climbs with the upgrade of size and light source.
Why is laser TV so expensive?
It must be for a reason.
With the continuous development of light source and technology, laser TV has many advantages that cannot be achieved by LCD TVs and projectors.
With ultra-short focal length projection technology, the projection distance of laser TV is shorter than that of traditional projection equipment, which can achieve a 100-inch large screen experience in a short distance, requires little space, and the main unit is small and occupies very little space.
In terms of brightness, big brand laser TVs can reach more than 2000 lumens.
In addition, it has lower heat generation and less equipment loss, so the life of TV is longer.
In addition, many high-end laser TV models are equipped with true 4K ultra-high definition, artificial intelligence system, bionic screen and many other cutting-edge technologies, which can bring users a more shocking audio-visual experience.
It is not a pseudo-demand, but a product of demand segmentation. In terms of picture quality and input-output ratio, laser TV is not suitable for audio-visual players. If you want to balance the large screen and insufficient viewing environment, you can try it. There are pros and cons to everything. After weighing the pros and cons, you can make your own judgment. Just be afraid of believing one-sided words.
In short, ultra-wide-angle near-focus projectors - laser TVs - have to use expensive ultra-wide-angle lenses and mirrors to get close to the screen, which is not in line with optical best practice.
In order to block some of the ambient light, microstructured Fresnel screens are used, but they are expensive, and the price of an 80-inch screen is basically equivalent to that of a 65-inch LCD.
One more thing, I really hate that projection manufacturers use the argument that diffused light is good for eyes as a selling point. Please, the light on the screen that our eyes can see is direct light. If you diffuse it to the ceiling, our eyes cannot see it. You can’t argue that LCD TVs are also diffused “direct light”. When you turn on the TV, the ceiling will be lit up, right?
And if the projection screen is not a diffuse reflection, it cannot be an image.
The hot spot (light spot) effect of high gain screens is overlooked, and it is really uncomfortable when a lot of light is reflected into the eyes at a certain angle.
