Is it reliable to buy insurance for small umbrellas?
Little Umbrella is an Internet insurance brokerage platform.
From the perspective of the conditions for establishment: according to the prompt of the Regulations on the Supervision of Insurance Brokerage Institutions (Revised in 2015), to become an insurance brokerage company, it must meet three key conditions: shareholders and sponsors must have no major illegal records in the past three years, the minimum limit is RMB50 million, and it must be approved by the China Insurance Regulatory Commission.
These three are actually very strict. In summary, they require strength, approval by the national authority of the China Banking and Insurance Regulatory Commission (which supervises insurance companies and banks nationwide), and good conduct by shareholders. As a small umbrella insurance company, the insured do not need to question whether or not small umbrella insurance is legitimate. After all, these three thresholds are not easy to meet for any insurance company.
It's not bad at all, and it's reliable.
From the insurance product perspective: Little Umbrella Insurance is an insurance supermarket. Many companies' star critical illness insurances are sold on this platform, such as the latest Super Mary No.5 Critical Illness Insurance launched by Huatai Life Insurance, where both the insurer and the reinsurer are Huatai Life Insurance, and Little Umbrella Insurance is just a third-party intermediary. There is no need to doubt its reliability.
From the payment mode: Internet platform products have lower operating costs and business employee salaries than physical outlets, so internet products generally have high cost-effectiveness. In addition, the payment method is flexible, supporting monthly payments to reduce the burden of insurance premiums for policyholders, and can maximize the leverage of insurance.
That's a very good thing.
It's worth considering.
From the perspective of the professionalism of the salesperson: this is particularly important because insurance is a highly professional industry. Many customers can purchase insurance themselves through Internet products, which can lead to many misunderstandings. In terms of health disclosure and occupation, non-insurance practitioners tend to add their own subjective consciousness when purchasing insurance, and feel that they are healthy or eligible. This can easily lead to many claims risks and may be rejected.
I myself have sold insurance by phone in “Tencent Micro Insurance” (an Internet insurance brokerage platform like Little Umbrella), and I can tell you that it is a very responsible job.
Internet insurance is real and reliable. What is not reliable is the people. Internet channels do not have professional practitioners, and there are many sales misleadings.
For example: 1 yuan insurance covers 6 million, buy insurance and get a mobile phone, and receive 60,000 yuan of major illness insurance for free. These marketing strategies are not wrong, but they avoid the main point. They will not tell the policyholder that the first month is 1 yuan, but the next month will be charged normally, ranging from a few dozen to 200 yuan. Many customers do not know what they have bought after paying for one or two years.
They thought they could get away with it for $1, but the insurance company paid out $6 million (a real windfall).
The free mobile phone is only available for special policy numbers, such as 6666 and 8888. And if the winning SMS is sent out, no one will remind you if you don't exchange it.
(For the insured, this is another pitfall) Free 60,000 yuan of major illness insurance is short-term. If you rely on this alone and have no other commercial insurance, what can you do if you really get sick? The 60,000 yuan will not solve the problem, and your life will start from scratch.
At present, the state is also cracking down on misleading sales of Internet insurance.
So in summary, the platform is fine and the product is fine, but the problem lies with the professionalism and ethics of the sales staff.
It is recommended that the author should consider carefully. In fact, many offline insurance brokerage companies have a wider range of products. Customers have more opportunities and products to choose from. Some products are more cost-effective than Internet products.
I have helped 59 Internet customers to insure their offline products, saving each person 2000-3000 yuan/year.
If you have any questions, please feel free to communicate with me.
I: Mingya Insurance Broker, with 3 years of enterprise management training experience, serving nearly 1,000 enterprise users, cooperating with more than 100 insurance companies, before insuring, I will start from customer needs, customize insurance products for customers, during insuring, I will compare three insurance companies for customers, try my best to get the best underwriting result for customers, after insuring, I will do policy arrangement and policy custody for customers, so that customers can rest assured.
Grow with each customer for life.
I hope my answer can help you!
According to the definition of insurance regulators, Internet insurance is an insurance business in which the insured can complete all the insurance procedures independently after clicking on the insurance link.
During this process, the insured does not need to sign, and the insurance advisor does not need to sign. All steps can be completed independently by the insured.
Although the application for Internet insurance does not have much "ritual sense", and there may even be no printed paper contract, this does not affect the validity and legal effect of the electronic contract.
This model is a bit like a call centre, but there is a fatal flaw in buying insurance in this way - the follow-up service can be poor.
Insurance is usually a long-term contract lasting for decades. When we buy insurance, should we think carefully about whether the "mediator" we choose can accompany us in the long run and provide services to us many years later?
